# What never enters a build context.
#
# There was no .dockerignore here until deploy/Dockerfile.test needed one, and every
# image in deploy/ (and sim/) builds with `context: ..` -- the repo ROOT, not the
# directory the Dockerfile lives in. So every `docker compose build` has been tarring up
# the whole checkout and sending it to the daemon before reading the first COPY, and
# the COPYs only ever take skill_monitor/ and pyproject.toml.
#
# Two entries below are the reason this file exists rather than being tidiness:
#
#   .git      2.2 MB of history that no image has ever needed.
#   .claude/  13 MB when this was written, and unbounded, because .claude/worktrees/
#             holds ENTIRE SECOND CHECKOUTS of this repo -- one per concurrent agent,
#             each with its own .git. .gitignore already refuses to commit them; that
#             does nothing for a build context, which is a filesystem walk and not a
#             git operation. Without this file a build from inside a worktree session
#             ships the other agents' branches into the daemon.
#
# Nothing here excludes anything an image COPYs. Adding skill_monitor/, pyproject.toml
# or specs to this file breaks every deployed image silently -- the build succeeds and
# the container starts without the package.
.git
.gitignore
.claude

# Build leftovers of the host's interpreter. On the test image these would be Windows
# CPython 3.13 bytecode arriving in a Linux 3.10 container, which is at best ignored;
# deploy/Dockerfile.test also sets PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE so the reverse cannot happen.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache

# Editor and environment state, which is per-machine by definition.
.vscode/
.idea/
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/

# Recordings and outputs. These are what the stacks mount at /data at RUN time; a copy
# baked into an image at build time is a stale duplicate of a volume, and episode logs
# are the one thing here that grows without a bound.
data/
output/
scratch/
*.jsonl
*.bag
